NAME
       uniq - remove or count repeated lines that follow each other

SYNOPSIS
       uniq [OPTION]... [INPUT [OUTPUT]]

DESCRIPTION
       uniq reads lines and prints them, folding a run of identical
       neighbouring lines into one. Equal lines that are not next to each
       other are not noticed, so sort the input first.

OPTIONS
       -c, --count
              Put the number of times each line occurred in front of it.

       -d, --repeated
              Print only lines that occurred more than once.

       -u, --unique
              Print only lines that occurred exactly once.

       -i, --ignore-case
              Ignore upper and lower case when comparing.

       -f, --skip-fields=N
              Skip the first N fields of each line when comparing.

EXAMPLES
       Count failed ssh logins per address, most first:

           grep 'Failed password' /var/log/secure | awk '{print $(NF-3)}' |
               sort | uniq -c | sort -nr

       Print lines that appear twice in a list:

           sort names.txt | uniq -d

SEE ALSO
       sort(1), wc(1), comm(1)
